# Copy to .env (in the repo root) and fill in the real values, then run:
#   docker compose up --build
#
# Docker Compose auto-loads this repo-root .env for ${VAR} substitution.
# Never commit the real .env — only this .example template is tracked.
#
# Five values are genuinely required: DATABASE_URL, DIRECT_URL,
# GATEWAY_ENCRYPTION_KEY, BETTER_AUTH_SECRET, and — in production —
# EMAIL_UNSUBSCRIBE_SECRET, which the API and worker both refuse to boot without.
# Everything else has a working default, and any variable you leave blank is
# treated as unset rather than as a deliberate override.

# ── Ports (change if one is already taken on your machine) ──────────────────
# These are the single source of truth for both Docker Compose's host mappings
# and plain `npm run dev`:
#   WEB_PORT   the Vite dev server binds it; `VITE_PORT` overrides it for a
#              worktree that needs its own server alongside another session's
#   API_PORT   `apps/api/server.ts` binds it, and the Vite dev proxy sends
#              `/api` to it (`PORT` still wins inside the container)
#   DOCS_PORT  the Docusaurus site
# Change one here and every side follows — nothing is hardcoded in the apps.
WEB_PORT=8080
API_PORT=3001
REDIS_PORT=6379
DOCS_PORT=3100

# ── Database — plain Postgres, no vendor features required ────────────────────
# Any managed Postgres (Neon, RDS, Supabase-as-DB) or a container you add to
# this file. Nothing here depends on who hosts it.
#
# DATABASE_URL is the runtime URL; where it is POOLED (pgbouncer, transaction
# mode, usually :6543 with ?pgbouncer=true), DIRECT_URL must be the DIRECT
# :5432 one — `prisma migrate deploy` cannot run through a transaction pooler.
# A raw `@` in the password must be URL-encoded to %40.
DATABASE_URL=postgresql://user:password@host:5432/acruxcore
DIRECT_URL=postgresql://user:password@host:5432/acruxcore

# ── Required secrets ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Generate each with a SEPARATE run of:  openssl rand -base64 32
#
# AES-256 master key for provider-credential encryption. The API refuses to boot
# without a valid base64-encoded 32-byte key. Rotating it makes every stored
# provider credential undecryptable, so keep it backed up.
GATEWAY_ENCRYPTION_KEY=
# Signs session cookies. REQUIRED in production — the API refuses to boot
# without it, so a deploy that forgets it crash-loops. Changing it invalidates
# every signed-in session, which is also how you force a global sign-out.
BETTER_AUTH_SECRET=

# ── Auth (all optional) ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# There is no identity vendor to configure: accounts, sessions, and password
# hashes all live in this deployment's own Postgres.
#
# Session lifetime in days. Default 7.
AUTH_SESSION_TTL_DAYS=
# Whether a new account must confirm its address before it can sign in. Left
# blank this follows the mail transport — on unless EMAIL_TRANSPORT=none, since
# an install that cannot send mail cannot ask anyone to confirm anything. Set it
# only to override that.
AUTH_REQUIRE_EMAIL_VERIFICATION=
# On an instance with zero users, the API logs a one-time claim link on every
# boot so the first owner can create their account. Set to `false` to disable.
AUTH_ALLOW_FIRST_RUN_CLAIM=
# Which reverse proxies sit in front of the API, as IPs or CIDR ranges. The
# forwarded chain is read right to left and the first hop NOT on this list is
# taken as the client address the rate limiter counts against. Left blank it
# defaults to the loopback and private ranges, which is right for this stack
# (nginx reaches the API over the Compose network) and for a host reverse proxy
# in front of it. Set it only if one of your proxies has a public address.
AUTH_TRUSTED_PROXIES=
# Headers checked, in order, for that client address. Blank means
# `cf-connecting-ip,x-forwarded-for`: Cloudflare rewrites the first on every
# request, and installs without Cloudflare never see it and use the second.
AUTH_IP_HEADERS=
# Optional "Continue with Google". Both or neither — half a pair shows no button
# instead of one that fails at the provider. Authorised redirect URI is
# <APP_URL>/api/v1/auth/callback/google.
GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=
GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET=

# ── Outbound product email ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
# ses    — AWS SES API. Needs SES_REGION + the two IAM keys.
# smtp   — any SMTP server, including SES's own SMTP endpoint. Needs SMTP_HOST
#          and SMTP_PORT. This is the usual self-host choice.
# memory — collects and logs instead of sending. Fine for a trial run.
# none   — no mail at all, and auth still works: accounts are created already
#          verified, the first-run claim link is logged, and
#          `npm run reset-password -w @acruxcore/api -- <email>` mints a reset
#          link on the box for anyone locked out.
#
# Whatever you pick applies to BOTH the api and worker services. The worker is
# what actually sends, so a mismatch means mail that queues and goes nowhere.
EMAIL_TRANSPORT=smtp

# Required when EMAIL_TRANSPORT=ses. Delete this block if you use `smtp` — every
# optional variable is defaulted to empty in docker-compose.yml, so leaving one
# out is silent, and carrying blank lines you never read is just noise.
#
# SES_REGION must be the region where your sending identity is VERIFIED, not
# whichever region is nearest. Identities are per-region, so pointing at the wrong
# one makes SES reject every message as an unverified domain — and that error is
# classified permanent, so nothing retries. If you already use the SMTP endpoint,
# the region is in its hostname: email-smtp.<region>.amazonaws.com.
#
# These IAM keys also need an `ses:SendEmail` policy attached. SMTP credentials
# generated in the SES console arrive pre-scoped for sending; a general-purpose
# IAM key does not, and the resulting AccessDenied is likewise not retried.
SES_REGION=
SES_ACCESS_KEY_ID=
SES_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=

# Required when EMAIL_TRANSPORT=smtp. Supply both credentials or neither — a
# relay that needs no auth is allowed. SMTP_SECURE=true means implicit TLS
# (port 465); leave it false for STARTTLS on 587.
#
# NOTE for SES users: SES SMTP credentials are NOT the IAM keys above. Generate
# them in the SES console (Account dashboard → SMTP settings → Create SMTP
# credentials); the SMTP password is derived from an IAM secret, not equal to
# it. Using an IAM key here fails to authenticate.
SMTP_HOST=
SMTP_PORT=587
SMTP_USER=
SMTP_PASSWORD=
SMTP_SECURE=false

EMAIL_FROM=acruxcore <no-reply@acruxcore.com>
EMAIL_REPLY_TO=support@acruxcore.com
# Base URL for every link inside an email, and the only origin auth accepts.
# The server has no window.location.origin. Must be the URL users actually
# visit, scheme included — a wrong value produces mail whose links 404.
APP_URL=http://localhost:8080
# Simultaneous send calls in the worker.
EMAIL_WORKER_CONCURRENCY=5
# HMAC key for one-click unsubscribe tokens — 32 random bytes, base64, and a
# DIFFERENT value from the two secrets above. REQUIRED in production: the API
# and worker both refuse to boot without it, because every notification email
# carries a link minted with it.
#   openssl rand -base64 32
EMAIL_UNSUBSCRIBE_SECRET=

# ── Weekly usage digest ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Kill switch. Defaults to true in production and false everywhere else, so a
# developer running the worker locally has no weekly job firing. Setting this to
# false in production stops the schedule on the next worker restart, no deploy.
DIGEST_ENABLED=false
# Cron pattern, always interpreted as UTC. Default: Mondays 08:00 UTC.
DIGEST_CRON=0 8 * * 1
# Simultaneous per-team digest builds (aggregate queries, not send calls).
DIGEST_WORKER_CONCURRENCY=2

# ── Sentry error monitoring (all optional) ───────────────────────────────────
# One DSN per service, from three separate Sentry projects — keeps a backend
# exception and a browser exception from ever landing in the same project's
# issue stream. Any left blank simply disables reporting for that service; none
# of the three are required to boot.
#
# SENTRY_WEB_DSN is baked into the web bundle at BUILD time (`docker compose
# build`/`up --build`), not read at container start — see apps/web/Dockerfile.
# Changing it requires rebuilding the web image, not just restarting it.
SENTRY_WEB_DSN=
SENTRY_API_DSN=
SENTRY_WORKERS_DSN=

# ── Google Analytics 4 (optional) ────────────────────────────────────────────
# Measurement ID for the marketing site + dashboard (e.g. G-XXXXXXXXXX). Also
# baked in at BUILD time like SENTRY_WEB_DSN above — same rebuild requirement.
# GA4's default cookie domain covers every acruxcore.com subdomain, so the
# same ID is reused for docs.acruxcore.com (see apps/docs's GA4_MEASUREMENT_ID
# GitHub Actions secret, wired in .github/workflows/docs.yml). The
# `analytics_storage` cookie itself only fires after a visitor accepts the
# on-site cookie banner (Consent Mode v2) — see apps/web/src/lib/analytics.ts.
# Left blank, analytics never loads.
GA4_MEASUREMENT_ID=
